WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.040
The idea of slowing down the ageing
process and living healthier,

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.080
more productive lives
is hugely appealing.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.080
It's led to a growing trend for
people looking to take control

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.000
of their own biology,
optimising their bodies

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.080
and minds through biohacking.

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.160
But how safe and ethical is it?

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.200
And are there more natural ways
of boosting your biology?

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.240
In this film, we're going to hear
from both the biohackers

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.240
trying to push human health
and wellbeing to its limits,

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.200
and those taking more traditional
scientific approaches.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:33.080
Dave Asprey is an American
entrepreneur

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.120
and the self-described
father of biohacking.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.160
I fully expect, and this
is not a made-up number,

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.000
to live to at least 180 years old

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
unless something like a tree
falling on me takes me out.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
A lot of people ask, can we really
hack the ageing process?

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.200
And we are now on the cusp,
with literally about 50

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.000
different innovations to control
biology of ageing coming online

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.200
all at the same time.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.200
And I have 100% certainty
that within a few years,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.240
our new innovations that come in
happen faster than our ageing.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.080
It's certainly true that the
scientific understanding of ageing

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.200
has progressed rapidly in recent
decades, but why is that?

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.000
I think one of the really big
surprises that's come along

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.160
about ageing
is how malleable it is.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.240
There are... Especially in animals.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.000
And I think what's come as
a huge surprise is that sometimes

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.000
the same thing will extend
the healthy lifespan of a fly

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.000
but also of a mouse.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.200
And what that's telling us,
of course, is that some

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.120
of the underlying mechanisms of
ageing that we're interfering with

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.040
are in common
between these animals.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:45.080
So I think that's why ageing has
opened up, really, to research

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.120
in recent years - the discovery that
we can use these short-lived things

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:52.200
where we can get the answers
more quickly to understand

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.160
what's going on in the creatures
that we really care about,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.080
particularly ourselves.

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:00.080
As the science of ageing and
longevity becomes more advanced,

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.200
we're learning how to
not just slow down gradually

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.160
the effects of ageing, but truly
reverse many aspects of ageing.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:12.000
We believe the cells in our bodies
have youthful information

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.160
when we're born and over time
they lose that information.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.080
We've discovered that
there's information in the cell

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.080
to reset the system in the way
that you could reboot a computer

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.240
with fresh software.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.080
For example, we've recently
reprogrammed the eye of an old mouse

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.040
to be young again and those
old mice get their vision back.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.080
And that's just the beginning,
I believe, of being able to turn

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.200
the clock back, literally, to make
younger tissues in the body.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.120
There are several companies within
the growing longevity industry

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.080
who are working on developing
regenerative therapies

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.040
to combat the diseases of ageing.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:52.000
One such company is Elevian,
who are investigating how a protein

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.160
found in young blood
might have surprising

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.120
regenerative capabilities.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:01.080
Our co-founders were part of a team
that really made some

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.120
important discoveries - that young
blood can rejuvenate aged animals

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:11.000
and vice versa - aged blood can
accelerate ageing in young animals,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.120
and out of that work, they
identified this little known factor

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.200
called GDF 11 that decreases with
age, and what we and other labs

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:26.120
have now found is that a very
similar dose range of GDF 11

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:30.000
can be used to treat

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.160
and prevent dozens
of age-related diseases.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.120
This research,
which originally began on mice,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.240
is still in its early
pre-clinical stage.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.160
Any drug development process
which arises from it

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:45.120
has to be thorough,
which can take years.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.080
Elevian estimate that they are
about a year away

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:50.200
from beginning human
clinical trials.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:53.200
For those impatient to slow down
their ageing process

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:56.080
and not keen to take part
in experimental trials,

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:59.200
there are steps that people
can take in their daily lives.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.200
In many ways, our fight during
ageing is in our own hands already.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:08.200
It's really clear that if you can,
you should take as much exercise

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.080
as is healthy,

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.000
and also eat a healthy diet
and not eat too much.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.080
Dr David Sinclair believes
certain stresses on the body

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.160
can actually be good for longevity.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.120
If you do the things that trick
the body into thinking

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.200
times could be tough -
short bouts of skipping meals,

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:30.080
so hunger, fasting,
but also losing your breath.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.000
So running on a treadmill, doing
a lot of heavy weight lifting.

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:39.120
We also think that these are another
way to promote the body's defences.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.240
And the combination of all of
those things actually is known

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:46.080
to extend lifespan by keeping
people healthier for longer.

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.080
Some biohackers are determined
to go further

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.000
and are willing to trial
experimental therapies

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:53.200
on their own bodies.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:58.000
I decided that I wanted to try
every extreme intervention I could -

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.080
the stuff that billionaires
are doing,

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.120
so that I could write about it
and so that I could benefit from it.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:07.080
I've had my bone marrow taken out,
spun down to get stem cells

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:10.000
and reintroduced the stem cells
throughout my body.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.200
There's 150 supplements a day
that are part of my regime

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:14.160
but no-one really needs to do that.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.080
I am a guinea pig to see
which ones work

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.040
and I'm constantly experimenting.

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:22.080
There are different degrees
of how far people want to go

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.080
to slow down their ageing process.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.200
The most advanced I've ever seen,

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:30.000
if you want to call it advanced,
was there are a few people

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.080
that have tried reprogramming
their body with gene therapy

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:38.000
and giving themselves hormones
that are delivered by DNA.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:41.080
To me, that's a risk.
I definitely wouldn't go there

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:44.000
because these effects
are irreversible,

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:47.120
and if there was
a cancer-causing issue,

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:50.200
then, really, you're not
going to live longer, right?

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.160
There is scepticism among many in
the mainstream scientific community

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:57.000
around the role that
biohackers can play

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.040
in better understanding our biology.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.200
Do the risks of this
self-experimentation

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:03.160
outweigh the potential rewards

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:06.080
or is there a middle ground
that could help to push

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.200
the boundaries of optimising
our health?

00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:13.000
The idea of allowing for...

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.200
..more use of experimental therapy
under informed consent

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.000
in a way that all data
must be shared

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:27.080
so that we as a community
can learn from it,

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.240
I think that's a really
exciting idea.

00:06:29.240 --> 00:06:34.000
Biohacking has become a global
movement and we are out there

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:36.120
asking the hard questions
and doing the things

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.200
that a lot of scientists
would like to do

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:40.160
but they're not allowed to do,

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.080
because we still have autonomy
over our own bodies.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:47.000
And when we talk about our results,
when we share them online,

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:49.200
scientists see them
and go, "Interesting",

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.080
and then that provides fuel
for studies.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.080
The problem becomes when
people start doing things

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.080
which are not evidence based.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.200
You know, for which the information
really just isn't out there.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:04.000
Then I think obviously, you know,
as with anything else,

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:05.200
it can get dangerous.

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:09.000
If you don't have the information
on which to base a decision

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:11.200
to do something but you go
ahead and do it, I mean,

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.200
that's risky by definition.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.200
I think somewhere in between there
is a happy intermediate.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.000
I'm a scientist so I like to see
what happens to my body

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.000
when I do X, Y or Z,
but of course I'm very careful.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.160
I measure what happens

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:29.000
and make sure that I'm hopefully
not doing myself any harm.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.000
Many of those trying to hack
the ageing process

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.000
are also interested in trying to
optimise their immune systems.

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.080
In fact, there are biohackers
experimenting with procedures

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.000
such as faecal transplants - taking
someone else's poo as a medicine

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.000
in an attempt to cure
a range of ills.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:51.080
Though less extreme, supermarkets
and pharmacy shelves

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:54.240
are heaving with so-called immune
boosting pills and supplements.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.160
But is it even possible to alter
how the body responds to disease

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.000
through consuming someone else's poo
or by eating a blueberry pill?

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.200
And what are the risks?

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.120
The immune system is part of
an interconnected system

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:13.000
running all the way through our
body, involving all our tissues,

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:17.040
from our skin all the way
through to our toes.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.040
And in fact, the gut has one of
the largest resident populations

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.080
of immune cells in our body.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:26.080
Most of the body's immune cells
are found in the lining

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.000
of the intestine, and the microbes
in our gut play a crucial role

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.200
in keeping those immune cells
of our body healthy.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:35.200
They do that by providing
the key chemical signals

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.080
that they get once they digest
the good foods that we eat.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.120
So a healthy gut microbiome
is obviously crucial

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.120
to a healthy immune system.

00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:48.000
The immune system is considered
to be an organ, but unlike every

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:51.120
other organ in your body, it's one
where components of the system

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:53.120
can migrate around the body.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:56.120
You don't expect that with cells
from your lungs or your spleen

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.160
or any other solid organ,
but the beauty of the immune system

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:02.000
is it has a property
of immuno-surveillance.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.080
Immunological surveillance is when
the cells of the immune system

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:08.160
come together to co-ordinate
a response to an external threat

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:10.040
such as disease.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:13.080
For example, your lymph nodes -
the glands that swell up

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:16.080
when you are sick - are a sign
of your immune system at work.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.240
We often hear messages encouraging
us to boost our immune system.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.120
So, could we just take a pill
to do that?

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:24.200
When you do meet
an infectious threat,

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.080
our immune response has to react
quickly to contain the threat

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:32.000
and eradicate it,
and you don't want it to stay on

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:34.200
because if it stays on,
it's damaging.

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.120
And that is what happens in allergy
and autoimmune disease,

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:42.080
which are overactivity
of the immune response.

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.080
So there is no wonder pill
that's going to restore and boost

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:51.000
one part of the immune response
to help us be super-powered

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:56.080
because it's also interconnected
and the risks of getting it wrong

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:58.080
are really quite dramatic.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:01.120
So, instead of boosting
our immune system,

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:03.200
we should focus on supporting it.

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:09.160
Everything that we know already
which is good for our health

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:11.200
will also benefit our immune system.

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:14.120
More exercise
taken at regular intervals

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:16.160
has been shown to boost
your immune function.

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:19.000
That's been shown
and that's published data.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.040
Unfortunately, the modern
lifestyle is not very good

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:23.200
for our immune system.

00:10:23.200 --> 00:10:29.120
Our diets of convenience foods and
fatty foods that are low in fibre

00:10:29.120 --> 00:10:33.200
alongside sedentary lifestyles
with little exercise,

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:37.200
high stress and not too much
sleep, too much screen time.

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.080
All of these combine
to make our immune response

00:10:41.080 --> 00:10:43.080
not work as it should.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.200
Getting enough sleep
and reducing stress

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:48.240
can help our immune system
operate at an optimal level.

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:51.160
And even though one blueberry
won't work miracles,

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:55.000
getting a variety of the right foods
is very important.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:58.120
To help your immunity, you need
to be feeding up your gut microbes,

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:01.200
and the best way to do this
is to make them more diverse,

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:03.120
give them more species.

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:07.080
And I recommend 30 different
types of plant a week,

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:10.000
which gives you all the fibre
you should need.

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:14.000
Secondly, you should pick plants
to eat that are high in polyphenols.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:17.120
These are the chemicals that
are naturally found in plants

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:20.080
and are actually rocket fuel
for your gut microbes.

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:23.080
So things like brightly
coloured berries,

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:27.120
strong flavours like coffee beans,
dark chocolate

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:30.080
and even red wine
contains polyphenols.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:34.000
So if what you eat and your gut is
so important for your immune system,

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:37.000
why are some people
transplanting other people's poo

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:39.000
into their own stomachs?

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:46.160
Faecal microbial transplant,
or, colloquially, a poo transplant,

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:51.160
is taking a sample of a stool
from a normal person

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:54.200
and putting it into someone
who's unwell.

00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:59.000
This has been done for several
decades for very bad infections

00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:01.160
of the bowel
like Clostridium difficile

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:05.120
and it cures 90% of people,
and it's since started to be used

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.120
on a number of other conditions.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:10.000
This is a fascinating area
of research

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:13.000
but definitely not to be tried
at home, says Tim.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:16.120
I think this is an exciting new form
of therapy, but it's always best

00:12:16.120 --> 00:12:19.240
to do this within the confines
of a medical clinic

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:22.000
where things can be supervised

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:26.000
and the risk of side-effects
or infection is extremely low.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.120
There are some people who take
risks and do do it at home

00:12:29.120 --> 00:12:32.120
and those risks will be
multiplied considerably.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:35.120
There's a lot of hype
about faecal transplants

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:38.080
and you mustn't believe people
who say that it works

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.000
in everything, in everybody,
it certainly doesn't,

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.160
and there are many failures
in clinical trials.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:47.000
One of the ways we can effectively
and safely manipulate

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:49.200
our immune responses
is through vaccination.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:53.000
What a vaccine is doing
is essentially giving you

00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:57.040
the parts of the germ that will
stimulate the immune response

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:00.000
without actually getting
the full-blown infection.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:03.040
Another thing that we can do
is actually try to switch

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:05.000
the immune response off.

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:09.080
For some diseases, we don't want
a powerful immune response

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.200
because that's what's causing
the damage.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.120
This is exactly what's done
in diseases

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:18.080
like rheumatoid arthritis,
multiple sclerosis,

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:22.080
or is also being investigated
in infections like Covid.

00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:25.120
The good news is, whilst there's
no one magic pill we can take

00:13:25.120 --> 00:13:28.120
to optimise our immune response,
the more scientists learn

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:31.120
about how our immune system works,
the more they can develop

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:35.080
new drug treatments and therapies
to combat a wide range of diseases.

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:39.120
The knowledge of science is actually
leading to all sorts of wonderful

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:44.080
new possibilities to help treat us
and win the war against infection.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:48.200
As well as optimising our bodies,

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.240
many of us are also interested
in boosting our brains.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.160
In fact, for centuries,
people have looked for ways

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:57.240
to increase their intelligence,
concentration and creativity

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:00.200
through nootropics,
or cognitive enhancers.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:03.080
If you're currently sipping
on a skinny latte,

00:14:03.080 --> 00:14:05.240
you're consuming a form
of nootropic right now.

00:14:05.240 --> 00:14:10.000
Caffeine is a stimulant and famous
for its ability to perk you up.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:13.120
But so-called smart drugs
are gaining popularity.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:16.120
There's now a huge market for
over-the-counter supplements

00:14:16.120 --> 00:14:19.080
which claim, with very little
scientific evidence,

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:21.040
to boost concentration and memory.

00:14:21.040 --> 00:14:25.120
Some people go even further and
seek out prescription stimulants,

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:29.000
such as modafinil, in a bid to
improve performance at work

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:30.120
or while studying.

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:33.080
A 2017 study based on
the Global Drug Survey,

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:37.080
an anonymous questionnaire,
showed that 30% of Americans

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.120
had taken some kind of smart drug
in the last 12 months.

00:14:40.120 --> 00:14:43.000
That was up from 20% in 2015.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:44.200
And they're not alone.

00:14:44.200 --> 00:14:48.000
Large increases were reported
across Europe as well.

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:51.240
But do these drugs actually work?
And what are the risks?

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:55.000
Hanneke den Ouden
is a cognitive neuroscientist

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:58.000
at the Donders Institute
for Brain Cognition and Behaviour

00:14:58.000 --> 00:14:59.160
at Radboud University.

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:02.160
Her lab has been studying how
the state of our brain's chemistry

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:04.040
shapes how we act.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:07.200
So, modafinil is in a category of
drugs classed as psychostimulants.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:12.000
Other examples are methylphenidate
and, for example, amphetamine.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.080
And psychostimulants act generally
in a way to enhance activity

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:16.240
in your dopamine system.

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:19.240
Raised levels of a neurotransmitter
called dopamine

00:15:19.240 --> 00:15:22.120
can stimulate signals
in the parts of your brain

00:15:22.120 --> 00:15:25.040
associated with concentration
and focus.

00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:29.000
It also helps to reduce hyperactive
and impulsive behaviours.

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:33.000
This is incredibly useful
for people with ADHD, for example,

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:36.120
but it's also finding its way
illegally into the hands

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:39.080
of those without a diagnosed
medical condition.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:42.200
In some recent studies, we've
looked at how psychostimulants

00:15:42.200 --> 00:15:46.000
affect decision making
in a young, healthy population,

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:50.000
so not patients who are taking
these for treatment.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:52.080
Specifically, we study
methylphenidate.

00:15:52.080 --> 00:15:54.120
This is a drug that's maybe
better known

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:56.200
by the brand name Ritalin
or Adderall.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:59.200
And what we found was that
methylphenidate makes you better

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:03.000
across a number of tasks,
and we think this might be the case

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:05.000
because we experience these tasks

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:07.120
as less, kind of,
cognitively effortful.

00:16:07.120 --> 00:16:10.120
But, says Hanneke, taking
these brain-boosting drugs

00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:13.000
without a medical prescription
is risky.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.200
All medications have a risk
of side-effects

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:18.160
and there's a reason that these
are prescription drugs.

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:22.240
So when we run a medication study,
for example, using psychostimulants,

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:25.120
we really extensively screen
our participants.

00:16:25.120 --> 00:16:28.080
We actually get a doctor to sign
off on prescribing people

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:31.160
even a single pill. We often
give people only a single dose.

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:34.120
We know, for example, that
psychostimulants increase

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:37.240
your heart rate, and this can pose a
risk for people who have underlying

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:41.040
heart problems like arrhythmia
that they may not even know about.

00:16:42.040 --> 00:16:44.160
Drugs don't affect everyone
in the same way.

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:47.000
Drugs will help some people
really well

00:16:47.000 --> 00:16:49.080
and they will not help others
at all.

00:16:49.080 --> 00:16:51.200
And this is really the Holy Grail
of psychiatry.

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:53.200
It's been for decades, probably.

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:56.240
How can we predict
what drugs work for whom?

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.160
Hanneke says there have been
virtually no studies

00:16:59.160 --> 00:17:02.200
on the long-term cognitive effects
of psychostimulant use

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:06.080
in non-patients, but boosting
the dopamine levels in the brain

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:08.160
could cause long-term problems.

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:13.080
So, I talked about this finely tuned
balance of our brain chemistry,

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:17.000
and when you disturb that system
by adding a lot of dopamine,

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:20.120
then the system may in response,
to try to regain that balance,

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:22.160
turn down its sensitivity
to dopamine.

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:26.080
And so you could become dependent
on taking the medication

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:29.200
to kind of get to your normal
levels of your system.

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:33.000
And then finally there's another
risk, and this is maybe even more

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:36.080
speculative but I think also
very important to think about,

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:39.080
is that being really focused
all the time

00:17:39.080 --> 00:17:42.200
is not necessarily the optimal
state for all situations.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:46.160
We do know that being super-focused
may actually reduce our creativity

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:51.040
and our openness to new ideas
or out-of-the-box solutions,

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:54.000
and so I think that we
don't want a society

00:17:54.000 --> 00:17:56.000
of just hyper-focused people.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.000
Rather than boosting your brain
power through medication,

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:01.120
there's growing evidence
that it can be boosted

00:18:01.120 --> 00:18:05.120
through more natural means -
namely meditation and mindfulness.

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:07.040
So how does that work?

00:18:07.040 --> 00:18:10.080
One consequence of being human is
the fact that we have these minds

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:12.120
that can kind of wander off
and think about

00:18:12.120 --> 00:18:15.000
all kinds of weird stuff, right?
This is Laurie Santos.

00:18:15.000 --> 00:18:17.040
She's a professor of psychology
at Yale

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:19.200
and teaches a course called
the Science of Well-Being.

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:22.200
The research shows that
the simple act of meditating,

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:25.080
even, like, ten minutes a day
if you're a novice,

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:27.120
can significantly reduce
brain activation

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:30.000
in regions of your brain
that tend to mind wander.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:33.080
So it's literally changing the
kind of default firing patterns

00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:36.080
of your brain just through
this simple act of meditating.

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:38.120
So why is mind wandering
not so good?

00:18:38.120 --> 00:18:41.080
Laurie says research shows
that when our mind wanders,

00:18:41.080 --> 00:18:43.040
it can make us unhappy.

00:18:43.040 --> 00:18:47.080
The irony is that we're never fully
present, right, and the enjoyment

00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:50.120
that we get out of simple actions
in life, whether that's eating

00:18:50.120 --> 00:18:53.160
something delicious or doing the
dishes or talking with a friend,

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:56.200
we really need to be present
for that to kind of kick in.

00:18:56.200 --> 00:18:59.080
We need to be there to savour it
and notice it.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:01.200
And this means that this act
of mind wandering

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:04.240
seems to significantly decrease
our wellbeing over time,

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:08.080
and that's why practices like
meditation can be so powerful.

00:19:08.080 --> 00:19:11.080
Meditation in a variety
of different forms.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.120
At least one of the benefits is
that it kind of trains our mind

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:16.240
to be a little bit more present
than is typical.

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:19.120
Not only does meditation
stop the mind wandering,

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:22.120
it also builds up more connections
between parts of the brain.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:25.200
It effectively rewires your brain
away from mind wandering

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:27.120
and towards the present.

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:31.200
And the effect lasts, according to
a 2008 study, which found people

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:35.160
who had practised meditation were
happier even eight weeks later.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:38.080
Meditation doesn't just
help us enjoy life.

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:42.040
A 2013 study by the University
of California showed that a course

00:19:42.040 --> 00:19:44.240
of meditation actually
boosted test scores.

00:19:44.240 --> 00:19:47.160
It also has a host of other
benefits, says Laurie.

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.120
It increases concentration.

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:52.160
It increases our memory over time.

00:19:52.160 --> 00:19:55.000
It has lots of physical health
consequences.

00:19:55.000 --> 00:19:58.120
You can see improvements in
immune function after meditation

00:19:58.120 --> 00:20:00.040
and things like that.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:03.080
You know, these kind of markers
of ageing and things like telomeres,

00:20:03.080 --> 00:20:05.160
those kinds of markers
seem to get better

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:07.120
for people who regularly meditate.

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:11.000
So if there are signs that it aids
your brain power, happiness,

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:13.080
immune function and even DNA,

00:20:13.080 --> 00:20:15.160
would it be too much
to talk about meditation

00:20:15.160 --> 00:20:18.040
as some kind of remedy
for the human condition?

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:20.120
Sometimes people talk
about this question -

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:22.080
is meditation sort of a panacea?

00:20:22.080 --> 00:20:24.240
And honestly, as a scientist,
we usually kind of get

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:28.000
a little worried when we see things
that have all these benefits,

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:31.040
but meditation just seems to be
one that, empirically speaking,

00:20:31.040 --> 00:20:33.080
just does have so, so many benefits.

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:37.080
So there you have it.

00:20:37.080 --> 00:20:40.120
It is possible to optimise
our bodies and our brains.

00:20:40.120 --> 00:20:44.000
Whilst DIY faecal transplants
and the use of non-prescribed

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:47.080
psychostimulants are definitely
not to be recommended,

00:20:47.080 --> 00:20:50.120
there are clearly other things
that can help many of us,

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.200
from vaccinations to meditation
to regular old exercise,

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:59.000
and even though living to 180
will probably not be attainable

00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:03.120
or even desirable, as the scientific
understanding of our bodies grows,

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:06.120
the hope is that many of us
will be able to look forward

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:09.200
to longer, healthier and happier
lives in the future.

